********  TCP and UDP ********


TCP - Transmission Control Protocol

it is very important for reliable data delivery

so to connect 1st it will send a connection request called sync signal

once server the syn signal and is ready to connect, it sends back sync+acknowledgment

and when client receivet it and is ready, client sends acknowledgment

        client  -----sync------->  server
                <---sync+ack-----  
                -----Ack--------->


so it is a 3 step connection which happens here and post that only transmission of data happen.

So its a 3 way handshake before transferring any data.

now once the data sync is done, transfer of data happens, it also maintains -numbering system of packets to maintain the order of the data

        P1 - P2 - P3 - P4  so packets are sent and sent in some order hence numbering is required


suppose we are downloading a file and some segments of data gor created and suppose we received segment1 , 2 and 3 and post that the connection broke.

so TCP knows that these many segments were sent so when connection is online again it carry on from there only.

-if any error appears it also does the error handling.

Where TCP is used?
-FTP (it relies on tcp to send large files)
-Email (email used SMTP and smtp also relies on Tcp to send email)
-Secure connection a it maintains proper handshake


advantages :
-Reliable
-Sends data in sequence
-Secure delivery

disadvantage
-it is slower than UDP

it is good for broad n/w but for smaller networks it is very hectic



UDP (User Datagram Protocol) :

Live streaming , in live stream we cannot rewind or go back correct?
because udp doesent manitain the connection prior sending the data and hence its Unreliable and Connection-less protocol.


